<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maritz Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maritz Rebellion was a rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> in 1914–1915 against the Union government's decision to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manie-maritz">Manie Maritz</a>, Christiaan de Wet, Christiaan Beyers and Jan Kemp, some 12,000 rebels took up arms but were defeated by government forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a> by early 1915. One rebel officer, Jopie Fourie, was executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maritz-rebellion.jpg" alt="Maritz Rebellion" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The British Army and The Illustrated London News · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Mushroom_Valley.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the First World War began in 1914, Botha’s decision to invade German South West Africa provoked the <b>Maritz Rebellion</b>, a rising of Afrikaner commandos under leaders such as Christiaan de Wet and Manie Maritz, which government forces suppressed by early 1915.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Military</a></li></ul></section>
